{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.SLeakMagnitudeTaylorRatio","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.SLeakMagnitudeTaylorRatio","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.SLeakMagnitudeTaylorRatio.linear_model_relative_error","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.SLeakMagnitudeTaylorRatio.linear_model_relative_error","kind":"theorem","line":106,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SLeakMagnitudeTaylorRatio.lean#L106-L118","doc":"**Linear-model relative error under the normalization `g(q*) = 1`.** With the\ncofactor normalized so that `g(q*) = 1`, the relative error of the leading linear\nmodel `L·(qp − q*)` against the exact `S(qp)` is exactly `g(qp) − g(q*)`, the\ncofactor's increment away from its value at the zero. This quantifies why one Taylor\norder in `g` buys several digits: the linear model's miss *is* the cofactor's own\ndeparture from the normalized `1` at `q*`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.SLeakMagnitudeTaylorRatio.offset_ratio_error_is_cofactor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.SLeakMagnitudeTaylorRatio.offset_ratio_error_is_cofactor","kind":"theorem","line":91,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SLeakMagnitudeTaylorRatio.lean#L91-L104","doc":"**The leading offset-ratio error is exactly the cofactor deviation.** The error\nof approximating the exact ratio `S(qp)/S(qm)` by the *leading* anchor-offset ratio\n`(qp − q*)/(qm − q*)` equals `(qp − q*)/(qm − q*) · (g(qp)/g(qm) − 1)`. This is the\nclosed form of the probe's **order-0 residual** (`≈ 6.18e-7`): the leading offset\nratio matches the exact magnitude only to the extent the cofactor ratio `g(qp)/g(qm)`\ndeparts from `1`, so each Taylor order restored to `g` shrinks the miss. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.SLeakMagnitudeTaylorRatio.exact_ratio_factorization","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.SLeakMagnitudeTaylorRatio.exact_ratio_factorization","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SLeakMagnitudeTaylorRatio.lean#L73-L89","doc":"**Exact ratio factorization (slope cancels, cofactor exact).** For a function\nwith the simple-zero factorization `S(q) = L·(q − q*)·g(q)` (`L ≠ 0`), and anchors\n`qm ≠ q*` with `g(qm) ≠ 0`, the exact ratio of two evaluations factors as the\nproduct of the anchor-offset ratio and the cofactor ratio:\n\n  `S(qp)/S(qm) = (qp − q*)/(qm − q*) · g(qp)/g(qm)`.\n\nThe slope `L` drops out entirely — sharpening `linear_ratio_slope_independent` from\nthe leading-order (`g ≡ 1`) case to the full cofactor. This is the identity the\nRoute-A probe evaluates order-by-order in the Taylor truncation of `g`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.SLeakMagnitudeTaylorRatio.cofactor_is_normalized_deviation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.SLeakMagnitudeTaylorRatio.cofactor_is_normalized_deviation","kind":"theorem","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SLeakMagnitudeTaylorRatio.lean#L59-L71","doc":"**The cofactor is the forced deviation from the linear model.** Given the\nfactorization `S(q) = L·(q − q*)·g(q)` of a function with a simple zero at `q*`\n(slope `L ≠ 0`), the cofactor `g` at any `qp ≠ q*` equals the multiplicative\ndeviation `S(qp) / (L·(qp − q*))` of `S` from its leading linear model. So `g` is\nnot a free ansatz — it is pinned by the factorization, and the normalization\n`g(q*) = 1` (used below) is the statement that this deviation is `1` at the zero. ","signatureChunk":0}]}